  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>The <strong>pituitary gland</strong> is a small, pea-sized gland at the base of the brain, sitting in a hollow of the bone called the sella turcica. It controls the thyroid, adrenal glands, sex glands, growth and metabolism.</p>
    <p>An <strong>adenoma</strong> is a benign pituitary tumour. There are:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>functioning</strong> adenomas — produce excess hormones (prolactin, growth hormone, ACTH and others);</li>
      <li><strong>non-functioning</strong> adenomas — produce no hormones but, as they grow, compress surrounding structures — above all the optic chiasm right above the pituitary.</li>
    </ul>
    <Scheme />
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Symptoms</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Vision problems</strong> — narrowing of the visual field at the sides ("tunnel vision"), reduced sharpness. A person may not notice this for a long time.</li>
      <li><strong>Hormonal disorders</strong> — depending on the adenoma type: menstrual problems and breast discharge, infertility, reduced potency; enlarged hands, feet and facial features; weight gain, raised blood pressure and blood sugar.</li>
      <li><strong>Headache</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>An adenoma is often found by chance, on an MRI done for another reason.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pituitary MRI with contrast</strong> — size of the adenoma and its relation to the optic nerves and carotid arteries.</li>
      <li><strong>Hormone tests</strong> — show whether the tumour is functioning and which hormone it produces.</li>
      <li><strong>Ophthalmologist examination</strong> with visual field testing.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Treatment of an adenoma is teamwork between a neurosurgeon, an endocrinologist and an ophthalmologist.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Treatment</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Observation</strong> — for small non-functioning adenomas that do not affect vision.</li>
      <li><strong>Medication</strong> — the main treatment for prolactinoma; prescribed by an endocrinologist.</li>
      <li><strong>Surgery</strong> — with optic nerve compression, functioning adenomas (except most prolactinomas), tumour growth.</li>
      <li><strong>Radiosurgery</strong> — for tumour remnants or recurrence.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="operaciya">
    <h2>Surgery through the nose</h2>
    <p>Most adenomas are removed <strong>endoscopically through the nose</strong> — by a transnasal transsphenoidal approach. An endoscope is passed through the nasal passage to the sella turcica, the thin bony wall is opened and the tumour is removed, freeing the optic nerves. There are no incisions on the face or head.</p>
    <p>I have completed special training in endoscopic transnasal and skull base surgery and perform these operations. Very large adenomas of complex shape sometimes require an open approach or a combination of methods.</p>
    <figure class="photo-fig">
      <img src="/media/navigation.webp" alt="Endoscopic surgery through the nose: endoscope view and neuronavigation on the monitors" width="1400" height="1050" loading="lazy">
      <figcaption>During endoscopic surgery: the endoscope view on the central monitor, neuronavigation based on MRI on the left.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section id="posle">
    <h2>After surgery</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>For the first weeks — do not blow your nose forcefully, lift heavy things or dive; a blocked nose is possible.</li>
      <li>Endocrinologist follow-up: hormone monitoring and replacement therapy if needed.</li>
      <li>Follow-up MRI — after a few months, then on schedule.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

